            <ArticleTitle>Is &quot;Early Total Care&quot; a Safe and Effective Alternative to &quot;Staged Protocol&quot; for the Treatment of Schatzker IV-VI Tibial Plateau Fractures in Patients Older Than 50 Years?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e400-e406</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BOT.0000000000000995</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare &quot;Early Total Care&quot; (ETC) with &quot;Staged Protocol&quot; (SP) for the treatment of Schatzker IV-VI tibial plateau fractures in patients older than 50 years regarding safety and effectiveness.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective cohort study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">An academic level 1 US trauma center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS/PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Eighty-one patients older than 50 years with Schatzker grade IV-VI tibial plateau fractures were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty-three patients were treated under SP with immediate external fixation followed by definitive internal fixation. Twenty-eight patients were treated under ETC with immediate internal fixation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Comparison of perioperative findings, time to bony and clinical union, soft-tissue and bony complications, radiological outcome, and secondary procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The 2 groups were comparable without significant difference regarding age, sex, side of involvement, body mass index, smoking status, American Society of Anesthesiologist classification, associated injuries, comorbidities, follow-up duration, and fracture classification. No statistically significant difference was found regarding the perioperative complications, quality of reduction, time to union, Rasmussen score at union or at the final follow-up, soft-tissue/bony complications, and the rate of the secondary procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">ETC seems to be a safe, efficacious, and effective alternative to the SP for the treatment of some Schatzker IV-VI fractures in patients older than 50 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Therapeutic Level III. See Instructions for Authors for a complete description of levels of evidence.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
